mhvtl for Debian
================

Differences from the upstream build
-----------------------------------

Tape media is stored in /var/lib/mhvtl instead of the upstream default of
/opt/mhvtl. Debian Policy 9.1.1 reserves /opt for software that is not
shipped by the distribution.

The kernel module is not built at package build time. It is shipped as
source in mhvtl-dkms and built by DKMS for the running kernel. Kernel
headers matching the running kernel must be installed, otherwise the DKMS
build fails and the module will be missing.

Starting the service
--------------------

The daemon instances are generated from /etc/mhvtl/device.conf by a systemd
generator, so the units are templates and are not enabled individually.
After editing the configuration, start everything with:

    systemctl start mhvtl.target

Run make_vtl_media(1) once to create the initial media if /var/lib/mhvtl is
empty.

Why the daemons run as root
---------------------------

vtltape and vtllibrary run as root. This is not an oversight, and the
following was checked before deciding:

The daemons create their own character device node with mknod("/dev/mhvtlN")
in chrdev_create(). The kernel module registers a dynamic character major
but never calls device_create(), so no uevent is emitted and udev cannot
create the nodes instead.

Granting only CAP_MKNOD to an unprivileged daemon is not enough: mknod also
needs write permission on /dev, which is root-owned and mode 0755, so the
call fails with EACCES before the capability is consulted. Adding
CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE makes it work but is equivalent to root for file access,
which defeats the purpose.

Running fully unprivileged is possible, because mknod on an existing path
returns EEXIST and chrdev_create() already treats that as success. It would
require a privileged step to create and chown the nodes before the daemons
start. That is a change to the shared systemd units rather than to the
packaging, so it has not been done here.

The units instead drop everything root is not needed for: NoNewPrivileges,
a CapabilityBoundingSet reduced to CAP_MKNOD, CAP_SYS_RESOURCE and
CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE, and the ProtectSystem, ProtectHome, ProtectKernel* and
Restrict* directives. PrivateDevices is deliberately left off because it
would replace /dev with a private instance and hide the mhvtl nodes.
